<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurasia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eurasian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurasia is the combined landmass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Its steppe belt and trade routes linked empires across vast distances, enabling the expansion of nomadic powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, and the exchange of goods and ideas through empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurasia.jpg" alt="Eurasia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keepscases · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurasia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the late thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries, when Mongol guarantees of safe conduct allowed merchants to travel across <b>Eurasia</b> on an unprecedented scale, benefited the khanate through tolls and taxes on goods passing through its territory, although its frequent wars with the Yuan and the Ilkhanate repeatedly disrupted…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…preserved northern India as a functioning centre of civilisation during the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, when much of <b>Eurasia</b> was devastated, and made Delhi a refuge for scholars, artisans and Sufis fleeing the Mongols; the Chishti Sufi order, led in Delhi by Nizamuddin Auliya, flourished under its rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungar Khanate was the last great independent nomadic empire of Inner Asia, and its contest with the Qing decided the political map of Central <b>Eurasia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its culture travels too: Georgian polyphony, the kvevri winemaking method listed by UNESCO in 2013, the 1.8-million-year-old Dmanisi hominins, the oldest in <b>Eurasia</b>, and a diplomatic campaign that has persuaded Israel, Japan, South Korea, Hungary and Lithuania to drop the Russian name 'Gruzia'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese silk, lacquerware, and iron goods moved westward while glass, gold, and horses moved east, creating an unprecedented sustained exchange of goods, technologies, and ideas across <b>Eurasia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rashid al-Din's Jami al-Tawarikh, written at the Ilkhanid court with Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants, is often called the first universal history of <b>Eurasia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
